{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793382723,"nonce":"+pASOofjgIQUdJQDRBkeN0/JXMo=","message":"Correction to the previous decision (darmie): browser builds stay the default preview (Ashui WASM already works in the editor preview; JS/html5 for Heaps, OpenFL, Flixel and Lime should be supported the same way), but the pixel-streaming tier is not just a desktop path or fallback. It is the basis of a product offering: a GPU-enabled cloud session. 784b568 is therefore back to normal priority and is now about a GPU session class, not only software GL. Design consequences to keep open: (1) transport: raw damage rectangles do not fit a WAN at full-screen rates (3.7 MB a frame at 1280x720); with a GPU the right transport is hardware video encode (NVENC, VAAPI or QSV) and the protocol's encoding field should grow a video codec value and stream chunks rather than rectangles; ashui.media.Player is the presenter for encoded video. (2) Rendering: EGL headless on the GPU (no X, no window), not software GL. (3) Isolation: MSB 0.7.11 has no GPU option on msb run, so a GPU class needs another backend or GPU passthrough (VFIO) and a different runner; to be decided with the runner owner. (4) Product: Entitlement gains a session class (cpu, gpu) with its own limits and metering (GPU minutes), kept apart from storage quota; the sync core's Entitlement and Limits are the place. (5) Cloud sync: a restored workspace must be able to start in either class from the same snapshot.","files":null}]}